The mechanism goal for margins: the ability to back a Window with an ncursesw
subwindow that represents its writable area, without the user-facing API
revealing that subwindow.

The idea: a window with a border needs all its writes to stay inside that
border. Doing that coordinate arithmetic at every call site is error-prone,
so the writable area (the area inside the border/margin) gets its own
ncursesw WINDOW*, inset in the parent window, and window member functions
operate on it. To the caller the window remains a single window that
conceptually includes its border/margin; the margin is immutable (set when
the window/margin is created) and normal writes never touch it.

What this goal delivered is the subwindow machinery itself: the research
into which ncurses functions exist for this (subwin/derwin and friends), and
the Window::subwin/derwin API - including the Margin overloads - with the
documented rule that subwindows must be destroyed before their parent.
Giving the (to be renamed, see goal 02.5) window class the margin-aware
behavior of the new terminal::Window class is what goals 02.5 and 03 are
about.
